<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Cell Biol</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Cell Biology</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0021-9525</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-8140</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">19806696</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2107347</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>THE EFFECT OF PUROMYCIN ON INTRANUCLEAR STEPS IN RIBOSOME BIOSYNTHESIS</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Soeiro</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Vaughan</surname><given-names>M. H.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Darnell</surname><given-names>J. E.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1d4b190N0x3b890f0">From the Department of Biochemistry, the Albert Einstein College of Medicine of Yeshiva University, New York 10461</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>1</month><year>1968</year></pub-date><volume>36</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>91</fpage><lpage>101</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>4</day><month>5</month><year>1967</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>14</day><month>9</month><year>1967</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1968 by The Rockefeller University Press</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> Inhibition of protein synthesis by puromycin (100 &#x003b3;/ml) is known to inhibit the synthesis of ribosomes. However, ribosomal precursor RNA (45S) continues to be synthesized, methylated, and processed. Cell fractionation studies revealed that, although the initial processing (45S &#x02192; 32S + 16S) occurs in the presence of puromycin, the 16S moiety is immediately degraded. No species of ribosomal RNA can be found to have emerged from the nucleolus. The RNA formed in the presence of puromycin is normal as judged by its ability to enter new ribosomal particles after puromycin is removed. This sequence of events is not a result of inhibition of protein synthesis, for cycloheximide, another inhibitor of protein synthesis, either alone or in combination with puromycin allows the completion of new ribosomes. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>